1
00:00:01,946 --> 00:00:02,375
All right.

2
00:00:02,375 --> 00:00:02,969
In this lecture

3
00:00:02,969 --> 00:00:06,632
we're going to talk about, Kubernetes
networking at a very high level.

4
00:00:06,929 --> 00:00:10,922
Understand, 
the architectural benefits that are there

5
00:00:10,922 --> 00:00:14,453
and the flexibility that you get by using,
Kubernetes networking.

6
00:00:15,047 --> 00:00:16,862
Again, this is just a primer.

7
00:00:16,862 --> 00:00:20,063
There's several books that have been
written about, Kubernetes networking,

8
00:00:20,426 --> 00:00:24,782
interesting reads and, different design
patterns are discovered in those.

9
00:00:25,739 --> 00:00:28,412
So fundamentally, the fundamental model

10
00:00:28,412 --> 00:00:32,372
that's used is a flat pod network,
which means

11
00:00:32,372 --> 00:00:35,441
if every pod gets a unique IP

12
00:00:35,441 --> 00:00:39,401
routable IP address
and that pods communicate

13
00:00:39,401 --> 00:00:43,295
directly with each other,
with no Nat in the middle.

14
00:00:43,295 --> 00:00:45,242
So container,

15
00:00:45,242 --> 00:00:49,862
sits on an individual pod, and I can have
multiple containers in the pod.

16
00:00:50,324 --> 00:00:50,522
Right.

17
00:00:50,522 --> 00:00:53,525
But a pod itself receives
a unique routable

18
00:00:53,525 --> 00:00:57,122
IP address
that is, is one of the key, tenants.

19
00:00:58,475 --> 00:00:59,432
The other

20
00:00:59,432 --> 00:01:04,250
other key tenants in here are service
abstraction, which means that I can define

21
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:08,738
a service,
independent of the IP addresses.

22
00:01:09,035 --> 00:01:13,754
This gives me the flexibility
of having services know about each other

23
00:01:13,754 --> 00:01:16,757
without having to map directly
to an IP address.

24
00:01:16,823 --> 00:01:19,727
That mapping is done on the back end,

25
00:01:19,727 --> 00:01:24,314
through your DNS or cluster IP, concept.

26
00:01:24,677 --> 00:01:25,634
Right.

27
00:01:25,634 --> 00:01:29,099
And then there are other key
aspect here is network policy.

28
00:01:29,099 --> 00:01:34,181
So network policy allows me to define pod
to pod traffic

29
00:01:34,511 --> 00:01:39,296
to namespaces, labels
other policies, port port addresses.

30
00:01:39,494 --> 00:01:44,411
This gives me the ability
to really enforce, security.

31
00:01:44,411 --> 00:01:46,325
I can lock it down very tightly.

32
00:01:46,325 --> 00:01:49,625
And it's all enforced by CNI plugins.

33
00:01:50,219 --> 00:01:53,486
Or EB, P4 layers.

34
00:01:54,542 --> 00:01:58,634
So the good thing about the CNI plugins,
there's lots of them out there.

35
00:01:59,855 --> 00:02:03,683
And, I can set up different network
topologies

36
00:02:03,782 --> 00:02:06,785
and design patterns based off of those.

37
00:02:07,709 --> 00:02:11,207
So really simple, example

38
00:02:11,207 --> 00:02:15,794
we talked about in previous lecture
on Kubernetes architecture.

39
00:02:15,794 --> 00:02:20,744
Go check that one out that every worker
node has something called a kubelet.

40
00:02:21,074 --> 00:02:24,539
This is really kind of a controller
on setting up.

41
00:02:24,902 --> 00:02:29,720
Or maybe it's an agent
that's run, by the master controllers,

42
00:02:30,116 --> 00:02:34,043
but it's really, setting up the pods

43
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:38,498
and, setting up the network
so that these pods can talk to each other.

44
00:02:38,828 --> 00:02:43,679
So simply put, it can add a CNI plug

45
00:02:43,679 --> 00:02:46,682
in, into the worker node.

46
00:02:46,781 --> 00:02:51,368
And, you'll want the same plug in
for all of your nodes, right.

47
00:02:51,698 --> 00:02:54,503
Because it's going to set up different,

48
00:02:54,503 --> 00:02:58,595
networking topologies
for you or networking, philosophies.

49
00:02:59,255 --> 00:03:03,644
And then what happens is when I add a pod,
a network

50
00:03:03,644 --> 00:03:08,297
namespace gets created
based off of those plug ins.

51
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:11,696
And remember,
every pod gets a Nick IP address

52
00:03:12,224 --> 00:03:14,732
and it sets up that virtual link.

53
00:03:14,732 --> 00:03:19,946
But between the virtual Ethernet device
and the Ethernet device that's running

54
00:03:19,946 --> 00:03:24,401
inside the pod, which is really kind of
like a little tiny virtual machine,

55
00:03:24,764 --> 00:03:28,460
but it is a namespace in there
where I can run multiple containers.

56
00:03:29,021 --> 00:03:29,879
Right?

57
00:03:29,879 --> 00:03:32,915
All of the configurability
is stored in your,

58
00:03:32,948 --> 00:03:37,865
the `/etc/cni/net.d` directory

59
00:03:37,865 --> 00:03:40,208
so you can find
all that information in there.

60
00:03:40,208 --> 00:03:43,739
And then also I can talk
to the host network through the bridge

61
00:03:43,739 --> 00:03:44,894
or an overlay network.

62
00:03:44,894 --> 00:03:49,184
Just like we find
in Docker and Docker Swarm.

63
00:03:50,438 --> 00:03:53,441
One of the
big differences that you should know about

64
00:03:53,639 --> 00:03:56,576
is the, ability

65
00:03:56,576 --> 00:04:00,173
to extend the network
easily in Kubernetes over Docker Swarm.

66
00:04:00,503 --> 00:04:03,473
Docker swarm is highly opinionated,

67
00:04:03,473 --> 00:04:07,730
which means it's easy to set up,
but you're stuck with what they chose.

68
00:04:08,126 --> 00:04:13,076
With Kubernetes, a little more set up,
but you can choose

69
00:04:13,076 --> 00:04:17,201
several different, 
networking topologies and configurability.

70
00:04:17,201 --> 00:04:20,204
You can get into really fine
grained, control.

71
00:04:22,481 --> 00:04:23,669
So we've already talked about

72
00:04:23,669 --> 00:04:26,672
some of the CNI plugins.

73
00:04:26,903 --> 00:04:29,543
I have Calico, which does BGP

74
00:04:29,543 --> 00:04:32,975
routing; Flannel,
which is probably the easiest to set up,

75
00:04:33,602 --> 00:04:37,298
which is a VXLAN overlay,
very similar to Docker Swarm.

76
00:04:37,529 --> 00:04:41,951
It's an overlay network,
with a net is a mesh overlay.

77
00:04:42,776 --> 00:04:45,779
And then I have Cilium, which uses eBPF.

78
00:04:46,043 --> 00:04:50,630
It can handle, level three two, level
seven networking and security.

79
00:04:50,993 --> 00:04:52,115
Level seven.

80
00:04:52,115 --> 00:04:53,105
You're going to see level

81
00:04:53,105 --> 00:04:56,174
seven use a lot with service meshes,
which we'll talk about later.

82
00:04:57,395 --> 00:05:00,068
On each node, I have CNI.

83
00:05:00,068 --> 00:05:04,589
And to create the network namespace
and the virtual Ethernet pair

84
00:05:04,589 --> 00:05:08,912
that's required for the container to talk
to, the host

85
00:05:08,912 --> 00:05:11,981
and to anything else
side of the container.

86
00:05:11,981 --> 00:05:15,446
Right. And that's, that's the goal here.

87
00:05:15,446 --> 00:05:19,967
Remember, every pod is assigned
an IP address and a root.

88
00:05:20,132 --> 00:05:23,036
And that's unique to that pod.

89
00:05:23,036 --> 00:05:25,016
So let's talk about the service
networking.

90
00:05:25,016 --> 00:05:29,141
So service
networking is at the application layer.

91
00:05:29,141 --> 00:05:33,859
So the idea here is that I can specify

92
00:05:33,859 --> 00:05:38,182
dependencies between services
and connectivity between services by name

93
00:05:38,611 --> 00:05:42,340
instead of by having to know
and resolve the name,

94
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:46,036
to an IP address that's handled for me
underneath the covers,

95
00:05:46,564 --> 00:05:50,524
from the service, mesh
that's been created.

96
00:05:51,349 --> 00:05:55,177
The important thing about this
is this gives me flexibility.

97
00:05:55,639 --> 00:05:59,632
And more specifically, reliability.

98
00:05:59,632 --> 00:06:01,216
If the service goes down,

99
00:06:01,216 --> 00:06:05,440
ends up on a different pod,
the IP address is going to change.

100
00:06:05,737 --> 00:06:07,486
I don't really care.

101
00:06:07,486 --> 00:06:10,687
Because all of the traffic
is going to that service mesh,

102
00:06:11,248 --> 00:06:14,680
which, it is going to route it
to the appropriate

103
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:17,683
IP address and the appropriate container
that's running.

104
00:06:18,475 --> 00:06:21,478
If you are debugging
an individual container,

105
00:06:21,907 --> 00:06:24,679
this can get a little hairy sometimes.

106
00:06:24,679 --> 00:06:27,088
Container running right.

107
00:06:27,088 --> 00:06:30,520
Because, if your connectivity goes down
and all of a sudden

108
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:36,493
you won't be getting messages anymore, 
and, and everything is highly resilient.

109
00:06:36,493 --> 00:06:37,912
So it will keep running

110
00:06:37,912 --> 00:06:42,433
by spinning up new pods or connecting new
pods, with, those new containers.

111
00:06:44,347 --> 00:06:44,644
Okay.

112
00:06:44,644 --> 00:06:48,472
Service networking, key concepts
here is a cluster IP.

113
00:06:48,736 --> 00:06:50,782
This is a virtual IP.

114
00:06:50,782 --> 00:06:53,356
That's accessible
only inside the cluster.

115
00:06:53,356 --> 00:06:58,141
This, is very similar to microservice,
segmentation

116
00:06:58,636 --> 00:07:01,507
in that only inside the cluster

117
00:07:01,507 --> 00:07:05,467
can you access the pods, individually.

118
00:07:05,467 --> 00:07:06,655
Pod pot.

119
00:07:06,655 --> 00:07:09,658
Right on on this cluster IP.

120
00:07:10,087 --> 00:07:12,265
Namespace that, that is there.

121
00:07:12,265 --> 00:07:16,027
Your kube proxy,
which we talked about in the,

122
00:07:16,027 --> 00:07:20,152
Kubernetes architecture
lecture, is running on the worker nodes.

123
00:07:20,152 --> 00:07:23,155
It's handling all of that, for you.

124
00:07:23,386 --> 00:07:23,947
Right.

125
00:07:23,947 --> 00:07:28,402
It also will handle load
balancing of traffic across, a service.

126
00:07:28,402 --> 00:07:32,395
If you've got a service
running on a single machine, it will,

127
00:07:32,395 --> 00:07:36,124
it will, load balance
across the pods on that machine.

128
00:07:36,355 --> 00:07:38,995
If it's across multiple machines,
it will do the same thing.

129
00:07:38,995 --> 00:07:42,229
It will forward things on a node port.

130
00:07:43,054 --> 00:07:48,004
Is the individual ports
that are accessible to that node.

131
00:07:48,004 --> 00:07:53,053
It's a static port on every single
node of that gives me external access.

132
00:07:54,472 --> 00:07:57,079
Load balancer, as you already know.

133
00:07:57,079 --> 00:08:03,085
Gives me a public IP address,
so people can send me requests.

134
00:08:03,085 --> 00:08:07,705
I can, I can
then send it inside, the, cluster.

135
00:08:08,167 --> 00:08:13,051
And most of this comes through an ingress
network, and there's lots of different,

136
00:08:13,051 --> 00:08:17,737
configurability
that is available through the ingress.

137
00:08:17,968 --> 00:08:20,773
And there's also ingress controllers
that you can use.

138
00:08:20,773 --> 00:08:24,502
You can plug in your all weather's
interconnects, traffic or homegrown one.

139
00:08:24,997 --> 00:08:28,693
This is different than Docker Swarm,
which has one baked in.

140
00:08:29,089 --> 00:08:33,181
And this one you can develop your own
and have very complex routing rules

141
00:08:33,181 --> 00:08:36,184
or whatever you want to do.

142
00:08:37,702 --> 00:08:38,758
Here's another thing

143
00:08:38,758 --> 00:08:41,761
that's been added is DNS and discovery.

144
00:08:42,289 --> 00:08:44,533
This is, really, critical.

145
00:08:44,533 --> 00:08:47,536
If you want your services
to know about each other.

146
00:08:47,701 --> 00:08:53,806
And, this, gives me ability
to, call from one service

147
00:08:53,806 --> 00:08:58,294
to another based off of a name or service
or attributes,

148
00:08:58,294 --> 00:09:03,475
on those names, depending on which, 
architecture that you're using.

149
00:09:03,475 --> 00:09:06,016
They all get mapped to this in Kubernetes.

150
00:09:06,016 --> 00:09:09,580
They got a Q cube, cube, a DNS,

151
00:09:10,042 --> 00:09:12,715
which, can handle

152
00:09:12,715 --> 00:09:15,850
all the in
cluster DNS, routing that you need.

153
00:09:17,995 --> 00:09:20,998
There's also headless services
that you can create,

154
00:09:21,295 --> 00:09:24,958
where there's no cluster IP on them.

155
00:09:24,958 --> 00:09:30,634
The DNS returns pod IPS directly
from the client side, load balancing.

156
00:09:30,634 --> 00:09:34,330
So I can circumvent the, load balancing.

157
00:09:34,330 --> 00:09:39,445
That's built into Kubernetes by doing,
by doing this, if I want to use

158
00:09:39,445 --> 00:09:42,646
an external load balancer, against,

159
00:09:42,646 --> 00:09:45,649
client pods that are sitting there.

160
00:09:47,068 --> 00:09:47,431
All right.

161
00:09:47,431 --> 00:09:51,853
Network policies,
just real quick, network policies,

162
00:09:51,853 --> 00:09:56,242
are highly configurable and that's what
you would expect with Kubernetes, right.

163
00:09:56,242 --> 00:09:59,410
Configure
configurability over convenience, right.

164
00:09:59,410 --> 00:10:02,446
Because I may want to lock down my cluster

165
00:10:02,446 --> 00:10:08,287
very tightly, in which I can say, pod
A can only talk to pod B through this

166
00:10:08,419 --> 00:10:12,379
set of ports,
in highly secure environments.

167
00:10:12,379 --> 00:10:13,996
You may want to do that.

168
00:10:13,996 --> 00:10:17,296
In other environments
where things are more dynamic

169
00:10:17,296 --> 00:10:21,619
and you need more resilience
than security, you may do some trade

170
00:10:21,619 --> 00:10:24,787
offs there on how tight
